"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"members a reader needs to build a graph by key.","type":"text"}],"type":"topic","title":"MutableGraph"},"doc://Hypergraphs/documentation/Hypergraphs/GraphProtocols/Hypergraph/dual()":{"url":"\/documentation\/hypergraphs\/graphprotocols\/hypergraph\/dual()","kind":"symbol","role":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/Hypergraphs\/documentation\/Hypergraphs\/GraphProtocols\/Hypergraph\/dual()","type":"topic","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The dual hypergraph (Berge’s H*, XGI’s and HyperNetX’s "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"dual()"},{"type":"text","text":"), the transpose of the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"incidence: its vertices are this hypergraph’s positions, in order, and its hyperedge "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"i"},{"type":"text","text":" is"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"vertex "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"i"},{"type":"text","text":"’s set of hyperedges. An isolated vertex becomes an empty hyperedge, and an empty"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"hyperedge an isolated vertex, so the dual is always defined; "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"dual().dual()"},{"type":"text","text":" is this"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"hypergraph with its vertices relabeled to their indices and its positions kept."}],"title":"dual()","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"func"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"dual"},{"kind":"text","text":"() -> "},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:11Hypergraphs23UndirectedIncidenceListV","kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"UndirectedIncidenceList"},{"kind":"text","text":"<"},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Self"},{"kind":"text","text":"."},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols10HypergraphP10HyperedgesQa","kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Hyperedges"},{"kind":"text","text":"."},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:Sl5IndexQa","kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Index"},{"kind":"text","text":">"}]},"doc://GraphProtocols/document